WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.680
Test, pack, price and grow your cleaning products range.

2
00:00:04.030 --> 00:00:09.450
This is the video for module 4: Quality Release, Packaging, Pricing and Scaling Up.

3
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:14.810
A product is ready to sell only when it passes tests you can repeat.

4
00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:20.260
You need pH strips, a scale, a funnel, a phone timer and a few clear bottles.

5
00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:25.410
For example, Funke checks her dish batch DL-07 before delivery.

6
00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.310
Appearance: clear yellow, matching her reference bottle. Pass.

7
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.830
pH strip: 7, inside her range of 6 to 8. Pass.

8
00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:42.260
Flow: 21 seconds, inside her target of 18 to 24. Pass.

9
00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:56.780
She weighs ten bottles. The average is 516.1 g, but one bottle is 514 g, below her 515 g minimum. She tops it up and re-checks five more.

10
00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:10.530
Her performance test passes, so batch DL-07 is released, and a retained sample goes on the shelf. When a customer complains, that sample and the batch record tell you what went wrong.

11
00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.690
Packaging must resist the chemical inside and protect people.

12
00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:19.750
HDPE suits acids, alkalis and disinfectants.

13
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:25.430
Acid and disinfectant products sold to homes should have child-resistant caps.

14
00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:31.750
Give each product its own colour band, so a cleaner can tell toilet gel from dish liquid at a glance.

15
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:42.280
Never use drink bottles. Children have swallowed chemicals from familiar bottles. And print your NAFDAC number only once you have it, never before.

16
00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:49.850
Every label carries the product name, the net content that matches your fill weight, and directions with the dose.

17
00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:55.640
It carries warnings and first aid, including do not mix with other products.

18
00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:14.940
And a batch code. For example, Ifeoma in Asaba codes her second dish batch made on 5 October 2026 as DL-261005-02. The same code goes on the batch sheet, test card, retained sample and every bottle.

19
00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:28.520
Many producers copy the price of the bottle next to theirs, then find out they were working for free. So cost every batch first. These naira prices are illustrative only. Use your own receipts.

20
00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:34.250
Chiamaka's raw materials come to 10,360 naira.

21
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:41.050
Her batch fills 19 full bottles, so packaging is 5,890 naira.

22
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:53.140
Labour is 4 hours at 1,000 naira. If you do not pay yourself, you cannot grow. Then a share of rent, transport and data: 1,500 naira.

23
00:02:54.390 --> 00:03:06.420
The batch costs 21,750 naira. Divided by 19 bottles, that is about 1,145 naira per bottle. Any price below that loses money.

24
00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:13.490
Survey three competing products and convert each to price per litre, so you compare like with like.

25
00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:26.240
Chiamaka sets a wholesale price of 1,700 naira. Her profit is 555 naira per bottle, or 10,550 naira per batch.

26
00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:33.660
Her retail price is 2,000 naira, which earns 16,250 naira per batch.

27
00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:44.100
A reseller who buys at 1,700 and sells at 2,000 earns 300 naira per bottle, so shops have a reason to stock her.

28
00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:52.630
Screenshots and SMS alerts can be faked. Confirm the money in your own bank or wallet app before you deliver.

29
00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:58.140
Growth usually comes from one or two institutions ordering every week.

30
00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:01.290
Scale with a schedule, not just a bigger drum.

31
00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:08.020
Make acid, alkaline and disinfectant products on different days, and wash drums between products.

32
00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:21.280
At 100 kg, a splash means more chemical and more heat. Keep the same order of addition, add caustic in more portions, and train every member in PPE and first aid.

33
00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:24.490
Many women grow faster together.

34
00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:30.560
For example, five women in a Lagos market group each use 8 kg of LABSA a month.

35
00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:37.670
Buying 40 kg together, with illustrative prices, they each save 2,400 naira a month.

36
00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:41.620
They agree in writing who receives it and how it is weighed out.

37
00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:43.880
To remember.

38
00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:47.260
Run the five checks on every batch and keep a sample.

39
00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:50.440
Pack in the right container, never a drink bottle.

40
00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.050
Cost every batch, including your labour.

41
00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:57.690
And as you grow, keep acids, alkalis and disinfectants apart.

42
00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:10.800
Now write a test card for one product, design one full label, cost and price one product from real receipts, and draft your one-week scale-up plan. Continue with the lessons below.
